#: ../clips.rst:61
msgid "Trimming & Slicing"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:62
msgid "OpenShot provides multiple ways to adjust the start and end trim positions of a clip (also known as trimming). The most common method is to click and drag the left or right edge of a clip. Trimming can be used to remove unwanted sections from the beginning or ending of a clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:66
msgid "To **slice** a clip into smaller sections, OpenShot offers several options, including dividing or slicing a clip at the play-head (*vertical playback line*) position. Trimming and slicing clips are powerful tools that allow users to rearrange sections of video and remove unwanted parts."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:70
msgid "Here is a list of all methods for cutting and/or trimming clips in OpenShot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:76
msgid "Trim & Slice Method"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:78
msgid "**Resizing Edge**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:78
msgid "Mouse over the edge of a clip and resize it by dragging **left** or **right**. The left edge of a clip can not be resized smaller than 0.0 (*i.e. the first frame of the file*). The right edge of a clip can not be resized larger than the duration of a file (*i.e. the last frame of a file*)."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:79
msgid "**Slice All**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:79
msgid "When the play-head overlaps multiple clips, right-click the play-head and choose :guilabel:`Slice All`. This will cut/slice all intersecting clips on all tracks. You can also use the keyboard shortcuts :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+K` to keep both sides, :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+J` to keep the left side, or :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+L` to keep the right side of the clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:83
msgid "**Slice Selected**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:83
msgid "When the play-head overlaps a clip, right-click the clip and choose :guilabel:`Slice`. This provides options to keep the left side, the right side, or both sides of the clip. Alternatively, use :kbd:`Ctrl+K` to keep both sides, :kbd:`Ctrl+J` to keep the left side, or :kbd:`Ctrl+L` to keep the right side."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:86
msgid "**Slice Selected (Ripple)**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:86
msgid "Slice the selected clip(s) at the play-head position, keeping either the left side (shortcut: :kbd:`W`) or the right side (shortcut: :kbd:`Q`), while rippling the gap removal across the current track."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:88
msgid "**Razor Tool**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:88
msgid "The :guilabel:`razor tool` from the **Timeline Toolbar** cuts a clip at the position where it is clicked. See :ref:`razor_tool_ref` for cut guides and frame previews. Use :kbd:`SHIFT` to slice and keep the left side, or :kbd:`CTRL` to keep the right side."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:90
msgid "**Split File Dialog**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:90
msgid "Right-click on a file and choose :guilabel:`Split File`. This opens a dialog that allows you to create multiple clips from a single video file."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:94
msgid "Keep in mind that the above cutting methods also have :ref:`keyboard_shortcut_ref`, to save even more time."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:96
msgid "If multiple selected clips and/or transitions share the same left edge or right edge, you can drag that shared edge once to trim them together."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:102
msgid "Razor Tool"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:104
msgid "Use the **Razor Tool** to preview a cut and split a clip or transition directly on the timeline. Each click affects only the item under the pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:107
msgid "Enable :guilabel:`Razor Tool` in the **Timeline Toolbar**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:108
msgid "Move over the clip you want to cut. A vertical dashed guide marks the exact frame boundary, with a timecode and the action that clicking will perform."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:110
msgid "While playback is paused, **Video Preview** shows the source frame of the hovered clip, including its time mapping. Higher tracks cannot obscure this preview. The playhead stays in place, and the guide's timecode is timeline time."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:113
msgid "Click to split. Razor stays enabled so you can make more cuts."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:114
msgid "Press :kbd:`Esc`, or toggle the toolbar button again, to return to normal editing."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:116
msgid "Moving away from the clip or leaving Razor mode restores the preview at the playhead. Hovering during playback does not interrupt playback. Like edge trimming, Razor previews the individual source rather than the composed timeline. Hovering a transition previews its mask source."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:121
msgid "Razor actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:125
msgid "Action"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:126
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:127
msgid "Click"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:128
msgid "Split at the guide and keep both sides. The previewed frame starts the right side."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:129
msgid ":kbd:`Shift+Click`"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:130
msgid "Keep the left side of the guide. The right side is shaded to show what will be removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:131
msgid ":kbd:`Ctrl+Click`"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:132
msgid "Keep the right side. The left side is shaded to show what will be removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:133
msgid ":kbd:`Alt+Shift+Click`"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:134
msgid "Keep the left side and close the gap, shifting later items on the same track left."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:135
msgid ":kbd:`Alt+Ctrl+Click`"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:136
msgid "Keep the right side and close the gap, shifting later items on the same track left."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:137
msgid ":kbd:`W` / :kbd:`Q` while hovering"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:138
msgid "Use the existing ripple-slice shortcuts at the pointer: keep left (:kbd:`W`) or right (:kbd:`Q`) and close the gap. Customized bindings work too."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:141
msgid "Holding :kbd:`Alt` with :kbd:`Shift` or :kbd:`Ctrl` adds **Close gap** to the hint. The red shading shows the portion being removed. These operations reuse **Slice Selected (Ripple)**; they move later clips and transitions on the same track, not on other tracks. Outside Razor hover, the ripple shortcuts retain their normal selected-item/playhead behavior. A locked or invalid Razor target does not fall back to cutting a different selected clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:148
msgid "Cuts align to project frames. With **Snapping** enabled, the Razor guide uses the same snap targets and distance tolerance as other timeline edits: clip and transition edges, markers, the playhead, and the hovered clip's keyframe targets. The hovered item's own endpoints are excluded. Preview, mouse cuts, and ripple shortcuts all use the guide's snapped boundary. Toggle the timeline's existing **Snapping** button to cut freely between these targets."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:155
msgid "Without snapping to a target, :kbd:`Shift+Click` includes the frame under the pointer in the left side, placing the guide at the following frame boundary. When snapped, the snapped boundary takes priority for all cut modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:159
msgid "Locked tracks, empty space, and boundaries that would leave an empty side do not show a valid cut guide. Zoom in for finer frame selection, and use **Undo** to reverse a cut."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:163
msgid "To cut several clips at the playhead instead, use **Slice Selected** or **Slice All**, described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:167
msgid "Clip controls while zoomed in"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:169
msgid "When a clip's start scrolls off screen, its title, effect badges, and menu stay near the left side of the visible timeline. Click an effect badge to access that effect, or use the title's dropdown to open the clip menu. The controls stay within the visible portion of their clip and shorten as space runs out."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:174
msgid "Only these controls follow the viewport. The clip's outline, thumbnails, waveform, and trim handles remain at their actual timeline positions. A small inset separates the pinned controls from the viewport edge."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:179
msgid "Selections"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:180
msgid "Selecting clips and transitions on the timeline is an essential part of editing in OpenShot. Multiple selection methods are available to streamline your workflow, enabling efficient editing of clips and transitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:183
msgid "Here is a list of all methods for selecting clips in OpenShot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:189
msgid "Selection Method"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:191
msgid "**Box Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:191
msgid "Click and drag a selection box around clips or transitions to select multiple items at once. Hold down :kbd:`Ctrl` to add to your current selection."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:192
msgid "**Click Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:192
msgid "Click on a clip or transition to select it. This will deselect all other items unless you hold down :kbd:`Ctrl`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:193
msgid "**Add to Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:193
msgid "Hold down :kbd:`Ctrl` while clicking to add or remove clips from the current selection, allowing you to select non-adjacent clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:194
msgid "**Range Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:194
msgid "Hold down :kbd:`Shift` while clicking to select a range of clips/transitions from the previous selection to the new selection. This supports ranges that span multiple tracks as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:195
msgid "**Ripple Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:195
msgid "Hold down :kbd:`Alt` while clicking to ripple select all clips/transitions from your selection to the end of the track. This always adds to your current selection, even if :kbd:`Ctrl` is not pressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:196
msgid "**Clear Selection**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:196
msgid "Click anywhere on the timeline or on a new clip/transition to reset the current selection, unless :kbd:`Ctrl` is pressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:197
msgid "**Select All**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:197
msgid "Press :kbd:`Ctrl+A` to select all clips and transitions on the timeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:198
msgid "**Select None**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:198
msgid "Press :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+A` to deselect all clips and transitions on the timeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:201
msgid "Mastering these selection techniques will streamline your editing process, especially when dealing with complex projects. For more advanced tips on selection and editing, refer to the :ref:`clips_cutting_slicing_ref` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:207
msgid "Context Menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:208
msgid "OpenShot has tons of great preset animations and clip properties, such as fading, sliding, zooming, reversing time, adjusting volume, etc... These presets can be accessed by right-clicking on a clip, revealing the context menu. A preset sets one (or more) clip properties for the user without needing to manually set the key-frame clip properties. See :ref:`clip_properties_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:213
msgid "Some presets allow the user to target either the start, end, or entire clip, and most presets allow the user to reset a specific clip property. For example, when using the :guilabel:`Audio → Volume` presets, the user has the following menu options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:217
msgid "**Reset Volume** — resets the volume to the original level."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:218
msgid "**Start of Clip** — the volume change applies at the beginning of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:219
msgid "**End of Clip** — the volume change applies at the end of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:220
msgid "**Entire Clip** — the volume change applies to the entire clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:228
msgid "Preset Name"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:230
#: ../clips.rst:618
msgid "Copy / Cut / Paste"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:230
msgid "Copy selected clip data, cut selected clips, or paste copied clip data. Copy supports full clips, effects, and keyframe groups."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:231
msgid "Align"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:231
msgid "Align the left or right edge of multiple selected clips and transitions (only shown when multiple clips are selected)."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:232
#: ../clips.rst:244
msgid "Fade"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:232
msgid "Fade the clip in or out — automatically fades video (alpha) and/or audio (volume) based on what the clip contains."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:233
#: ../clips.rst:261
msgid "Motion"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:233
msgid "Add animated motion to a clip: slide in/out, bounce, blur, wipe/focus-wipe, zoom, emphasis effects at the playhead, camera movements, and scrolling credits. Only shown for visual clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:234
#: ../clips.rst:496
msgid "Transform"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:234
msgid "Apply geometric presets to a clip: rotate/flip, crop, or snap to a corner layout. Also provides a **No Transform** reset. Only shown for visual clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:235
#: ../clips.rst:397
msgid "Look"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:235
msgid "Apply visual style presets: color grades, film grain, analog tape, sharpen, blur, shadow, and glow effects. Includes **Adjust Colors** (Color Wheels editor) and **Analyze Colors** (video scopes). Only shown for visual clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:236
#: ../clips.rst:328
msgid "Speed"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:236
msgid "Reverse, repeat, speed up, or slow down video. Includes Freeze and Freeze & Zoom options."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:237
#: ../clips.rst:444
msgid "Audio"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:237
msgid "Control audio properties: adjust volume levels, fade volume in/out, separate audio channels, show/hide the timeline waveform, or open the Audio Levels scope."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:238
#: ../clips.rst:471
msgid "Slice"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:238
msgid "Cut the clip at the play-head position, with options to keep either side, both sides, or ripple-delete one side. Only shown when the playhead overlaps the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:239
#: ../clips.rst:610
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:239
msgid "Show the properties panel for a clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:240
#: ../clips.rst:642
msgid "Remove Clip"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:240
msgid "Remove a clip from the timeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:245
msgid "The :guilabel:`Fade` preset creates a smooth fade-in or fade-out on the selected clip. It automatically fades **video** (alpha/opacity) and/or **audio** (volume) depending on what the clip contains — a video clip gets both, an audio-only clip gets only a volume fade, and an image clip gets only an alpha fade."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:249
msgid ":guilabel:`No Fade` — removes all fade keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:250
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade In` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — fades from transparent/silent at the start of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:251
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade Out` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — fades to transparent/silent at the end of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:252
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade In and Out` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — applies both a fade-in at the start and a fade-out at the end."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:254
msgid "Fast fades span approximately 1 second; Slow fades span approximately 3 seconds. See :ref:`clip_alpha_ref` and :ref:`clip_volume_ref` key-frames."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:257
msgid "**Usage Example:** Applying a fade-out to a video clip to gently conclude a scene."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:258
msgid "**Tip:** Fade and Volume fade are independent — use :guilabel:`Fade` for a combined video+audio fade, or use :guilabel:`Audio → Volume` to fade only the audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:262
msgid "The :guilabel:`Motion` menu adds animated movement to visual clips using keyframe presets. It is organized into several submenus. See :ref:`clip_location_x_ref` and :ref:`clip_scale_x_ref` key-frames."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:265
msgid ":guilabel:`No Motion` — removes all motion keyframes from the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:266
msgid ":guilabel:`In` — entrance animations applied at the start of the clip:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:268
msgid "**Back In** (From Bottom / Left / Right / Top) — clip overshoots and springs back into position."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:269
msgid "**Blur In** — clip fades in from a motion blur."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:270
msgid "**Bounce In** (Center / From Bottom / Left / Right / Top) — clip bounces as it enters."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:271
msgid "**Focus Wipe In** (Circle Expand / Circle Shrink / From Bottom / Left / Right / Top) — clip is revealed by a wipe while sharpening from blur into focus."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:272
msgid "**Pop In** — clip scales up quickly from nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:273
msgid "**Slide In** (From Bottom / Left / Right / Top) — clip slides onto the screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:274
msgid "**Spiral In** — clip rotates in while scaling up."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:275
msgid "**Wipe In** (Circle Expand / Circle Shrink / From Bottom / Left / Right / Top) — clip is revealed by a wipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:277
msgid ":guilabel:`Out` — exit animations applied at the end of the clip (mirrors the In options: Back Out, Blur Out, Bounce Out, Focus Wipe Out, Pop Out, Slide Out, Spiral Out, Wipe Out)."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:279
msgid ":guilabel:`Emphasis` — short attention-grabbing animations **inserted at the current playhead position** (not applied across the whole clip). Useful for mid-clip highlights: **Bounce**, **Flash**, **Heartbeat**, **Jello**, **Pulse**, **Rubber Band**, **Shake X**, **Shake Y**, **Swing**, **Tada**, **Wobble**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:281
msgid ":guilabel:`Camera` — simulates camera movement on the clip by animating scale and position:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:283
msgid "**Zoom** → In / Out — a simple push-in or pull-out."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:284
msgid "**Pan** → Auto Direction / Left to Right / Right to Left / Top to Bottom / Bottom to Top — a lateral camera pan. *Auto Direction* picks the best direction based on the clip's aspect ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:285
msgid "**Zoom & Pan** → In or Out, with directional variants — combines a zoom with a pan (Ken Burns style). *Auto Direction* picks the best direction based on the clip's aspect ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:287
msgid ":guilabel:`Credits` — scrolling credit animations: **Scroll Up** and **Scroll Down**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:289
msgid "**Tip:** Emphasis presets are placed at the current playhead position, so position the playhead over the moment you want to highlight before applying one."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:290
msgid "**Tip:** Camera presets use *Auto Direction* by default, which picks the optimal pan direction for wide or tall media to avoid black bars."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:295
msgid "Rotate"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:296
msgid "The :guilabel:`Transform → Rotate` submenu introduces easy rotation and flipping of clips. It enables orientation adjustment by rotating and flipping a clip for creative visual transformations. See :ref:`clip_rotation_ref` key-frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:300
msgid ":guilabel:`No Rotation` — removes any rotation keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:301
msgid ":guilabel:`Rotate 90 (Right)` — rotates the clip 90 degrees clockwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:302
msgid ":guilabel:`Rotate 90 (Left)` — rotates the clip 90 degrees counterclockwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:303
msgid ":guilabel:`Rotate 180 (Flip)` — flips the clip upside down."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:305
msgid "**Usage Example:** Rotating a photo or video by 90 degrees (a portrait video to a landscape)."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:306
msgid "**Usage Example:** If your video is upside down, rotate it by 180 degrees to correct the orientation."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:311
msgid "Layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:312
msgid "The :guilabel:`Transform → Layout` submenu adjusts the size of a clip and snaps it to a chosen corner of the screen, useful for picture-in-picture or watermark effects. See :ref:`clip_location_x_ref`, :ref:`clip_scale_x_ref`, :guilabel:`Margin`, and :guilabel:`Corner Radius` key-frames."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:317
msgid ":guilabel:`Reset Layout` — removes any layout keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:318
msgid ":guilabel:`1/4 Size` — positions the clip at 1/4 of the screen in the Center, Top Left, Top Right, Bottom Left, or Bottom Right corner."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:319
msgid ":guilabel:`Show All (Maintain Ratio)` — fits the entire clip frame within the screen while preserving its aspect ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:320
msgid ":guilabel:`Show All (Distort)` — stretches the entire clip frame to fill the screen, ignoring aspect ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:322
msgid "**Usage Example:** Placing a logo in the corner of a video using the layout preset."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:323
msgid "**Tip:** Use :guilabel:`Margin` to create an inset layout area for a clip, and :guilabel:`Corner Radius` to round picture-in-picture camera clips without adding a Mask effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:325
msgid "**Tip:** Combine with animation presets for dynamic transitions involving resizing and repositioning."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:329
msgid "The :guilabel:`Speed` menu manipulates clip playback speed, allowing for reverse playback, time-lapse, slow-motion, freezes, and looping effects. See :ref:`clip_time_ref` key-frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:332
msgid ":guilabel:`Reset Speed` — restores the clip to normal 1× speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:333
msgid ":guilabel:`Reverse` — plays the clip backwards at normal speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:334
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed Up` → Forward / Backward → 2×, 4×, 8×, 16× — speeds up playback in either direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:335
msgid ":guilabel:`Slow Down` → Forward / Backward → 1/2×, 1/4×, 1/8×, 1/16× — slows down playback in either direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:336
msgid ":guilabel:`Repeat` — see below."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:337
msgid ":guilabel:`Freeze` — freezes on the frame at the current playhead position for 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 20, or 30 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:338
msgid ":guilabel:`Freeze && Zoom` — freezes and simultaneously zooms in on that frozen frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:340
msgid "**Slow motion** — :guilabel:`Slow Down → 1/2×` or :guilabel:`1/4×` for dreamy or dramatic footage."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:341
msgid "**Time-lapse** — :guilabel:`Speed Up → 8×` or :guilabel:`16×` to compress hours into seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:342
msgid "**Speed ramp** — use the :guilabel:`Timing` tool to drag clip edges and create a natural-feeling speed change; OpenShot scales all keyframes automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:343
msgid "**Tip:** Combine :guilabel:`Freeze` with :guilabel:`Speed Up` for an impact-freeze-then-fast-forward effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:348
msgid "Repeat"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:349
msgid "Use :guilabel:`Speed → Repeat` to play a clip multiple times, without building the time curve by hand. OpenShot writes the needed :guilabel:`Time` keyframes for you (you can edit them later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:352
msgid "**Menu path**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:354
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed → Repeat → Loop → Forward` – plays left to right, then starts again from the beginning"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:355
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed → Repeat → Loop → Reverse` – plays right to left, then starts again from the end"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:356
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed → Repeat → Ping-Pong → Forward` – forward, then backward, then forward…"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:357
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed → Repeat → Ping-Pong → Reverse` – backward, then forward, then backward…"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:358
msgid ":guilabel:`Custom…` – opens a dialog for extra options (see below)"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:360
msgid "Counts are **finite** (2x, 3x, 4x, 5x, 8x, 10x, or a custom number). Example: “Forward then Back and stop” = :guilabel:`Ping-Pong → Forward → 2x`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:363
msgid "**What gets repeated**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:365
msgid "Repeat always acts on the **currently trimmed** in/out of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:366
msgid "The :guilabel:`Time` curve is stamped as a simple shape: - Loop Forward = rising saw - Loop Reverse = falling saw - Ping-Pong = triangle (direction flips each pass)"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:370
msgid "To avoid a double frame at the seam, the last frame of each pass is not duplicated."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:372
msgid "**Keyframes during Repeat**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:374
msgid "When you use Repeat, OpenShot will also **repeat other keyframes** found inside the trimmed section (location, scale, effects, etc.) into each pass so your animations stay in sync."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:375
msgid "Repeated keyframes keep their relative timing inside each pass."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:377
msgid "**Custom Repeat** (dialog options)"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:379
msgid ":guilabel:`Pattern:` Loop | Ping-Pong"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:380
msgid ":guilabel:`Direction:` Forward | Reverse"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:381
msgid ":guilabel:`Passes:` integer (2 or more). This is the number of times it will play."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:382
msgid ":guilabel:`Delay:` number + units [frames | ms | sec]. This is an optional delay between each repeated pass."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:383
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed Ramp (%):` % speed change per pass (optional). Positive speeds up each pass; negative slows down each pass."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:385
msgid "**Reset**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:387
msgid ":guilabel:`Speed → Reset Speed` completely removes any Time curve (including Repeat) and restores the clip to its original playback, **without deleting your original non-Time keyframes**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:391
msgid "Timing Tool"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:392
msgid "Another way to change a clip's speed is with the :guilabel:`Timing` tool on the timeline toolbar. Enable the clock icon and drag a clip's edges. Lengthening the clip slows playback, while shotening it speeds the clip up. All keyframes on the clip and its effects are scaled so their relative positions remain intact."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:398
msgid "The :guilabel:`Look` menu applies one-click visual style presets to clips. All presets work by adding or updating effects on the clip — you can inspect or animate them further in the Properties dock. The menu is organized into four submenus plus two direct actions at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:402
msgid ":guilabel:`Reset Look` — removes all Look-managed effects (Color Grade, Film Grain, Analog Tape, Sharpen, Blur, Shadow, Glow) from the selected clips in a single step."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:405
msgid "**Color** — applies a :guilabel:`Color Grade` effect with one of four quick presets:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:407
msgid "**Auto Contrast** — boosts contrast by lifting shadows and pulling highlights."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:408
msgid "**Lift Shadows** — brightens the dark areas of the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:409
msgid "**Warm Up** — shifts the color balance toward warm orange/amber tones."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:410
msgid "**Boost Color** — increases color saturation."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:412
msgid "**Film** — cinematic film simulation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:414
msgid ":guilabel:`Film Grain` → **No Film Grain**, then: **35mm Fine**, **35mm Classic**, **35mm Gritty**, **16mm Classic**, **Super 8**, **High ISO** — adds photographic grain at various strengths and sizes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:416
msgid ":guilabel:`Analog Tape` → **No Analog Tape**, then: **Subtle**, **VHS**, **Heavy** — adds tape noise and color degradation for a retro video look."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:419
msgid "**Focus** — sharpness adjustments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:421
msgid ":guilabel:`Sharpen` → **No Sharpen**, then: **Subtle**, **Medium**, **Strong** — sharpens fine detail."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:422
msgid ":guilabel:`Blur` → **No Blur**, then: **Soft Focus**, **Medium**, **Heavy** — softens the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:424
msgid "**Lighting** — light and shadow overlays:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:426
msgid ":guilabel:`Shadow` → **No Shadow**, then: **Subtle**, **Soft**, **Strong**, **Long** — casts a drop shadow across the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:428
msgid ":guilabel:`Glow` → **No Glow**, then: **Soft White**, **Warm**, **Neon**, **Inner Glow** — adds a soft halo or glow effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:431
msgid "At the bottom of the Look menu:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:433
msgid ":guilabel:`Adjust Colors` — opens the :guilabel:`Color Wheels` dock for full manual color grading with lift/gamma/gain wheels, curves, and an Amount/Luma blend slider."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:435
msgid ":guilabel:`Analyze Colors` — opens the :guilabel:`Luma Waveform` and :guilabel:`Histogram` video scopes on the right side of the window, tabified together."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:438
msgid "**Tip:** Each Look submenu has a \"No …\" option at the top to remove just that single effect without affecting the others."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:440
msgid "**Tip:** After applying a Look preset, double-click the effect badge on the clip (or use Properties) to fine-tune every parameter or animate it over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:445
msgid "The :guilabel:`Audio` menu groups all audio-related clip actions in one place."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:447
msgid "**Volume** — controls the clip's audio level. See :ref:`clip_volume_ref` key-frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:449
msgid ":guilabel:`Reset Volume` — removes all volume keyframes, returning to full (1×) volume."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:450
msgid ":guilabel:`Level` — sets a fixed volume percentage (0 % to 130 %) for the entire clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:451
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade In` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — fades volume from silence at the start of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:452
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade Out` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — fades volume to silence at the end of the clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:453
msgid ":guilabel:`Fade In and Out` → :guilabel:`Fast` / :guilabel:`Slow` — applies both a volume fade-in and fade-out."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:455
msgid "**Separate** — splits the audio track out of a clip:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:457
msgid ":guilabel:`Single Clip (all channels)` — creates one detached audio clip on a layer below the original."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:458
msgid ":guilabel:`Multiple Clips (each channel)` — creates separate detached audio clips, one per audio channel, on multiple layers below the original."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:460
msgid "**Show Waveform / Hide Waveform** — toggles the audio waveform visualization on the timeline for audio-only clips. Only shown for clips that do not have a video track."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:463
msgid "**Analyze Levels** — opens the :guilabel:`Audio Levels` scope dock. If other scope docks are already open, OpenShot tabifies :guilabel:`Audio Levels` with them."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:466
msgid "**Usage Example:** Applying a gradual volume fade-out to transition between scenes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:467
msgid "**Usage Example:** Extracting background music from a video clip for independent volume control."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:468
msgid "**Tip:** Use :guilabel:`Audio → Volume → Fade In/Out` for audio-only fades. Use the top-level :guilabel:`Fade` menu for a combined video+audio fade."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:472
msgid "The :guilabel:`Slice` tool lets you cut a clip at the position of the play-head (*the vertical line that shows your current position in the timeline*). This will split the clip into two separate parts at the exact point where the play-head is."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:475
msgid "Slicing is a key feature for making precise edits and rearranging sections of your video. You can slice a clip and choose to keep one side or both, and with the ripple option, you can automatically shift other clips on the same track to fill any gaps caused by the cut."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:479
msgid "**Slicing Options:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:481
msgid "**Keep Both Sides**: This option splits the clip into two parts, keeping everything on both sides of the play-head. It’s useful when you want to break a clip into sections but don’t want to remove anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:482
msgid "**Keep Left Side**: This option cuts the clip and removes the part to the right of the play-head, keeping only the part before the play-head. Use this to get rid of the portion of the clip that comes after the current point."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:483
msgid "**Keep Right Side**: This option cuts the clip and removes the part to the left of the play-head, keeping only the part after the play-head. It’s handy for trimming away the beginning of a clip and keeping the rest."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:484
msgid "**Ripple Slicing**: Ripple slicing not only cuts the clip but also moves any clips and transitions that follow to close the gap. This way, your timeline stays continuous without empty spaces after a cut, saving you the trouble of manually adjusting the clips that come after."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:486
#: ../clips.rst:660
msgid "**Tips for Beginners:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:488
msgid "**Example**: If there’s a part of a clip you don’t want (like the end of a scene), use **Keep Left Side** or **Keep Right Side** to remove it. If you want to split a scene into multiple smaller sections to rearrange, use **Keep Both Sides**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:489
msgid "**Quick Tip**: Slicing can also be used to break a long clip into smaller parts, making it easier to manage and edit each section separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:491
msgid "For a complete guide to slicing and all available keyboard shortcuts, see the :ref:`clips_cutting_slicing_ref` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:497
msgid "The :guilabel:`Transform` context menu provides geometric preset actions for visual clips:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:499
msgid ":guilabel:`No Transform` — removes all Rotate, Layout, and crop presets from the selected clips in one step."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:500
msgid ":guilabel:`Rotate` — rotate or flip the clip. See the :ref:`Rotate <clip_presets_rotate_ref>` section above."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:501
msgid ":guilabel:`Crop` — add or remove a crop effect. See the :ref:`Crop <clip_presets_crop_ref>` section below."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:502
msgid ":guilabel:`Layout` — resize and snap the clip to a corner or fit position. See the :ref:`Layout <clip_presets_layout_ref>` section above."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:507
msgid "Transform Tool"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:508
msgid "The **Transform Tool** lets you quickly adjust a clip directly in the preview window, instead of changing location, scale, rotation, shear, and rotation origin values one property at a time. OpenShot shows the Transform Tool automatically whenever you select a clip on the timeline, with blue lines and handles drawn around the selected clip. You can also select multiple clips at once with :kbd:`Ctrl` or :kbd:`Shift`; in that case, the preview shows one set of handles around the full selection, and any move, scale, or rotation is applied to all selected clips together."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:517
msgid "Dragging the blue squares will adjust the **scale** of the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:518
msgid "Dragging the center will move the **location** of the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:519
msgid "Dragging the mouse on the outside of the blue lines will **rotate** the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:520
msgid "Hold :kbd:`Ctrl` or :kbd:`Shift` while rotating to snap to 15-degree increments."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:521
msgid "Dragging along the blue lines will **shear** the image in that direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:522
msgid "Dragging the circle in the middle will move the **origin point** that controls the center of **rotation**."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:524
msgid "Note: Pay close attention to the play-head position (red playback line). Key frames are automatically created at the current playback position, to help quickly create animations. If you want to transform a clip with **no animation**, be sure the playhead is positioned before (to the left) of your clip. You can also manually adjust these same clip properties in the property editor, see :ref:`clip_properties_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:531
msgid "**Usage Example:** Use the transform handles to resize and reposition a clip for a picture-in-picture effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:532
msgid "**Tip:** Use these handles to precisely control a clip's appearance."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:537
msgid "Crop"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:538
msgid "The :guilabel:`Transform → Crop` submenu adds a crop effect to the selected clip and displays interactive crop handles in the video preview. The submenu offers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:541
msgid ":guilabel:`No Crop` – remove any existing crop effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:542
msgid ":guilabel:`Crop (No Resize)` – trim the clip without scaling the remaining area."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:543
msgid ":guilabel:`Crop (Resize)` – trim the clip and scale the cropped region to fill the frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:545
msgid "Drag the blue handles to adjust the crop boundaries, move the cropped area around, or move the center handle to reposition the image inside the cropped area."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:549
#: ../clips.rst:734
#: ../clips.rst:1067
msgid "Waveform"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:550
msgid "The :guilabel:`Audio → Show Waveform` / :guilabel:`Hide Waveform` action toggles whether an audio-only clip shows its waveform visualization on the timeline instead of the default thumbnail. This option only appears for clips that have no video track."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:554
msgid "**Usage Example:** Displaying the audio waveform for precise audio editing and alignment."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:555
msgid "**Tip:** Use this to visually spot loud or quiet sections in a music clip without opening the audio scopes."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:557
msgid "**How timeline waveforms are scaled**"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:559
msgid "Timeline waveforms are an editing aid: they help you find speech, beats, silence, and transients, and compare the approximate levels of different clips. New waveform data preserves the source's **absolute peak level**. OpenShot does not enlarge every quiet file until its highest peak fills the clip, so a quiet recording remains visibly quieter than a loud recording."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:564
msgid "Pure linear amplitude is accurate but makes ordinary speech difficult to see. OpenShot therefore applies a fixed, gentle square-root display curve. This expands useful detail while preserving the order of levels: a louder source always appears taller than a quieter source. The curve changes only the drawing; it does not alter, normalize, or process the recorded audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:569
msgid "Approximate waveform height for new clips"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:573
msgid "Peak Level"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:573
msgid "Display Height"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:573
msgid "What It Often Looks Like"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:575
msgid "``0 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:575
msgid "100%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:575
msgid "Full-scale peak; check for possible clipping."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:576
msgid "``-6 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:576
msgid "71%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:576
msgid "Strong transient or loud program audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:577
msgid "``-12 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:577
msgid "50%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:577
msgid "Healthy speech or other prominent audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:578
msgid "``-20 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:578
msgid "32%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:578
msgid "Moderate speech or background program audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:579
msgid "``-40 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:579
msgid "10%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:579
msgid "Quiet room tone or microphone noise floor."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:580
msgid "``-60 dBFS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:580
msgid "3%"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:580
msgid "Very quiet detail near the center line."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:583
msgid "OpenShot samples both **peak** and **RMS** envelopes at regular time intervals. The lighter outer envelope shows brief peaks and transients; the solid inner envelope shows sustained RMS energy. Both follow the clip's Volume and Time curves and use the same fixed display curve. A subtle center line keeps silent passages identifiable without making them look louder than they are."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:588
msgid "New waveforms use 200 samples per second by default, providing several measurements within a typical video frame. Change this density with :guilabel:`Edit → Preferences → Timeline → Waveform Samples (per second)`. Higher values show more detail when zoomed in but increase project data size and analysis time. The painter reduces dense data to the visible timeline pixels, and its rendered-waveform cache keeps normal scrolling and repainting inexpensive. Live microphone previews use the selected density and the same peak/RMS display as completed recordings."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:594
msgid "Projects saved by older OpenShot versions can contain independently peak-normalized waveform data. These waveforms have no format marker, so OpenShot deliberately displays them with the original legacy scaling. They remain familiar instead of becoming unexpectedly thin. Older waveform data without a stored density defaults to its original 20 samples per second, and data without an RMS envelope remains a single solid waveform. A project may safely contain legacy and new waveforms together."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:600
msgid "To regenerate cached waveform data using the current method, choose :guilabel:`Edit → Clear → Waveform Display Data`, then show the waveform again. This affects only cached display data and does not modify the media file or its audio."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:605
msgid "The peak envelope identifies transient level and possible clipping; RMS summarizes sustained energy but is not a loudness-standard measurement. Use the :guilabel:`Audio Levels` scope and your ears when mixing. See :ref:`playback_ref` for audio troubleshooting."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:611
msgid "The :guilabel:`Properties` preset opens the properties panel for a clip, allowing quick access for adjustments to clip properties, such as location, scale, rotation, etc... See :ref:`clip_properties_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:614
msgid "**Usage Example:** Adjusting clip properties like opacity, volume, or position."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:615
msgid "**Tip:** Apply this preset to streamline adjustments to all clip properties in a single dock."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:619
msgid "The :guilabel:`Copy / Paste` preset allows copying and pasting keyframes, effects, or duplicating an entire clip along with its keyframes. Paste will create a new clip at the position of your mouse. If you select 1 or more clips before pasting, you can paste \"over\" those clips with your current clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:623
msgid "**Usage Example:** Duplicating a clip with intricate animations for reuse in different parts of the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:624
msgid "**Tip:** Use this preset to replicate animations or effects across multiple clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:625
msgid "**Tip:** Selecting multiple clips before pasting, sets keyframes and/or effects for all clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:626
msgid "**Tip:** You can copy a single effect, and paste it to multiple selected clips."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:629
msgid "Remove Gaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:630
msgid "The :guilabel:`Remove Gap` and :guilabel:`Remove All Gaps` options help you quickly eliminate gaps between clips on the timeline by rippling (i.e., shifting) subsequent clips to close the gap. These options can be accessed via the context menu and are only available when gaps are detected."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:633
msgid "**Remove Gap:** - This option deletes a specific gap between two clips on the timeline. Right-click on the gap between clips to access the :guilabel:`Remove Gap` option. - Usage: Use this option to quickly eliminate a specific gap caused by trimming or using the razor tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:637
msgid "**Remove All Gaps:** - This option removes all gaps between clips on the timeline for the entire track. Right-click on the track name to access the :guilabel:`Remove All Gaps` option. - Usage: Ideal for tracks that contain back-to-back clips, such as a photo slideshow, where no gaps are desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:643
msgid "The :guilabel:`Remove Clip` option lets you delete a clip from the timeline. Removing clips is an essential part of organizing your project and getting rid of unwanted sections. Removing a clip can also impact the surrounding clips. If you want to clean up the gap that remains after deleting a clip, you have a few options to automatically adjust your timeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:647
msgid "**How to Remove a Clip:** To delete a clip, simply select it and press :kbd:`Delete` on your keyboard, or right-click the clip and choose :guilabel:`Remove Clip` from the context menu. You can also select multiple clips at once by holding down the :kbd:`Ctrl` key and clicking on additional clips, then remove them all at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:651
msgid "**Ripple Delete:** If you want to delete a clip and automatically remove the empty space (gap) it leaves behind, use the **Ripple Delete** feature by pressing :kbd:`Shift+Delete`. This will shift all the remaining clips and transitions on the track to the left, filling in the gap and keeping your timeline smooth and continuous."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:656
msgid "**Remove Gap:** After deleting a clip, if you have gaps left in your timeline that you want to remove, simply right-click in the empty space and choose :guilabel:`Remove Gap`. This action will shift all clips and transitions to the left, closing the gap and maintaining the flow of your video."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:662
msgid "**Example**: If you have a clip that’s no longer needed, such as an intro you’ve decided not to use, you can quickly select it and either delete it or use **Ripple Delete** to remove it and shift everything left to close the gap."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:664
msgid "For more advanced editing options and shortcuts, refer to the :ref:`clips_cutting_slicing_ref` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:667
msgid "Effects"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:668
msgid "In addition to the many clip properties which can be animated and adjusted, you can also drop an effect directly onto a clip from the effects dock. Each effect is represented by a small colored letter icon. Clicking the effect icon will populate the properties of that effect, and allow you to edit (and animate) them. For the full list of effects, see :ref:`effects_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:678
msgid "Clip Properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:679
msgid "Below is a list of clip properties which can be edited, and in most cases, animated over time. To view a clip's properties, right click and choose :guilabel:`Properties`. The property editor will appear, where you can change these properties. You can select multiple clips, transitions or effects at once. The dropdown at the top of the dock shows entries for each item plus an entry such as ``2 Selections``. Pick that entry to edit all selected items together, only properties they share will be visible. If a field is blank, the values differ between items, but you can still change it or insert a keyframe for all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:687
msgid "Note: Pay close attention to where the play-head (i.e. red playback line) is. Key frames are automatically created at the current playback position, to help quickly create animations."
msgstr ""

#: ../clips.rst:690
msgid "When animating clip properties, you can fade a clip from opaque to transparent with ``alpha``, slide a clip around the screen with ``location_x`` and ``location_y``, scale a clip smaller or larger with the ``scale_x`` and ``scale_y``, fade the volume of a clip quieter or louder with ``volume``, and much more. If you want to set a single, static clip property with **no animation**, be sure the playhead is positioned at the start of your clip (to the left) when adjusting the property value."
msgstr ""

